+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report for `salsa`
+
+Scope: `salsa` 0.28.1 (workspace root), tested with `hegeltest` 0.28.2 added as a
+dev-dependency (`cargo add --dev hegeltest -p salsa --ignore-rust-version`; salsa
+pins `rust-version = 1.85`, hegeltest's MSRV is 1.86, and the repo's pinned
+toolchain is 1.97, so this only affects the dev-dependency's declared MSRV).
+
+All tests were added to the existing test files for the code they exercise, per
+the skill.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+1. **Crown: incremental recomputation equals from-scratch computation**
+   (`tests/hello_world.rs::incremental_computation_matches_fresh_db`,
+   `#[hegel::state_machine]`). A group of `i64` input cells with three layers of
+   derived arithmetic queries (`cell_doubled` -> `group_sum_of_doubled` /
+   `group_max` -> `group_summary`). Rules: set a cell's value (full `i64` range,
+   optionally with a drawn durability, including `NEVER_CHANGE` which freezes the
+   field per its docs), add/remove cells (structure changes via a `Vec<Cell>`
+   input field), `synthetic_write` at drawn durability, warm individual memos,
+   and a check rule that compares every derived value against a fresh
+   `DatabaseImpl` built from the model state. Same-value writes are drawn with
+   boosted probability to exercise backdating.
+   *Evidence*: the `execute`/`red_herring` unit tests in the same file pin single
+   instances of exactly this contract.
+
+2. **Derived sums stay correct across arbitrary durability changes**
+   (`tests/durability.rs::add3_matches_sum_across_durability_changes`).
+   Generalizes the `durable_to_less_durable` regression test in the same file:
+   any initial durabilities, any sequence of value/durability sets (including
+   re-setting the current value at a different durability — the original
+   regression's shape), with queries only sometimes interleaved so validation
+   must cross skipped revisions. Values bounded to `u32::MAX / 3` to protect the
+   fixture's unchecked `+` (commented as such).
+
+3. **Accumulated values always reflect current inputs, in execution order**
+   (`tests/accumulate.rs::accumulated_logs_match_current_inputs`). After any
+   mutation sequence on `field_a`/`field_b`, `push_logs::accumulated::<Log>`
+   must equal exactly the `log_a(i of a)` entries followed by `log_b(i of b)`
+   entries for the current values (covering the a=0 / b=0 "function not called
+   at all" paths). *Evidence*: `accumulate_once` and the `change_a_*` tests pin
+   the ordering and staleness contract.
+
+4. **Incremental fixpoint (cycle) recomputation equals from-scratch computation**
+   (`tests/dataflow.rs::incremental_dataflow_matches_fresh_db`,
+   `#[hegel::state_machine]`). Arbitrary def/use graphs — including cycles
+   resolved by fixpoint iteration — mutated by changing increments, base edges,
+   and reaching-definition lists; a check rule compares `infer_use` /
+   `infer_definition` for every node against a fresh database built from the
+   model graph, queried in canonical order. **This property found a real bug**
+   (see Suspected bugs). After pinning the bug, mutations that would remove a
+   node from a cycle are skipped (`no_cycle_broken`, commented with a reference
+   to the canonical failing test); cycle-*creating* and cycle-*preserving*
+   mutations remain fully covered.
+
+5. **Interned data round-trips** (`tests/interned-structs.rs::interned_data_roundtrips`).
+   `InternedString::new(db, s).data(db) == s` for arbitrary Unicode strings.
+
+6. **Interned identity holds iff data is equal**
+   (`tests/interned-structs.rs::interned_identity_iff_equal_data`). For
+   two-field interned structs: handles are equal exactly when the field tuples
+   are equal (equal draws boosted), including the field-boundary case (same
+   concatenated content split at a drawn char boundary), generalizing
+   `interning_returns_equal_keys_for_equal_data_multi_field`'s
+   `("Hello, World", "")` example.
+
+7. **LRU eviction never changes query results**
+   (`tests/lru.rs::lru_eviction_never_changes_results`, `#[hegel::state_machine]`).
+   Arbitrary interleaving of input creation, field mutation, capacity changes
+   (including 0 = disabled), synthetic writes (which trigger eviction), and
+   queries of the LRU-limited `get_hot_potato` and its dependent
+   `get_hot_potato2`; results must always match the current input value.
+   *Evidence*: `lru_keeps_dependency_info` ("even though we've evicted it (LRU),
+   we find that it is still good").
+
+8. **Default cycle handling panics iff a cycle is reachable; acyclic values are
+   correct** (`tests/cycle.rs::default_cycle_handling_panics_iff_cycle_reachable`).
+   A tracked `node_height` function with default (panic) cycle strategy over an
+   arbitrary successor graph: if the model detects a cycle reachable from the
+   root, evaluation must panic with the documented "dependency graph cycle"
+   message (`src/function/fetch.rs`); otherwise it must return the model DFS
+   height without panicking.
+
+## Test results
+
+- All eight properties compile and run under `cargo test -p salsa` with default
+  hegel settings (100 cases each). Seven pass; the dataflow property passes
+  *after* the known-bug exclusion described above.
+- One pinned failing test (deliberately kept failing, deterministic, 0 draws):
+  `tests/dataflow.rs::known_failure_backdate_violation_when_cycle_is_broken` —
+  fails on every run in debug builds at `src/function/backdate.rs:76`.
+- Liveness check: temporarily inverted the `group_max` assertion in the crown
+  machine; hegel failed it and shrank to a minimal 1-step sequence
+  (`Step 1: check_against_fresh_db`). Reverted, and `.hegel/` deleted afterwards.
+- Exploratory 10x run: all eight properties run with `test_cases = 1000`; no
+  additional failures (dataflow machine: 15.5s at 1000 cases; everything else
+  under 3s). Reverted to defaults for the committed tests.
+- Bug discovery: the dataflow machine failed on its very first default-count
+  run; hegel shrank it to a 6-step sequence over a 1-def/1-use graph, from which
+  the 3-revision minimal reproducer below was derived by hand and verified.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### Backdate violation (`changed_at` moves backwards) when a fixpoint cycle is broken while values stay equal
+
+**Minimal counterexample** (pinned as
+`tests/dataflow.rs::known_failure_backdate_violation_when_cycle_is_broken`,
+fails deterministically in debug builds):
+
+```rust
+let mut db = salsa::DatabaseImpl::new();
+let def = Definition::new(&db, None, 0);   // increment 0, no base
+let u = Use::new(&db, vec![]);
+def.set_base(&mut db).to(Some(u));          // R2
+infer_use(&db, u);                          // Bottom
+infer_definition(&db, def);                 // Bottom
+u.set_reaching_definitions(&mut db).to(vec![def]); // R3: cycle u <-> def
+infer_use(&db, u);                          // fixpoint converges to Bottom
+infer_definition(&db, def);                 // Bottom
+u.set_reaching_definitions(&mut db).to(vec![]);    // R4: cycle broken
+infer_use(&db, u);                          // Bottom
+infer_definition(&db, def);                 // PANICS (debug builds)
+```
+
+Panic: `query infer_definition(Id(0)) returned the same value, but the previous
+execution changed at R3 and the new execution changed at R2. ... If the query
+has no untracked reads, please open a Salsa issue.`
+
+**Why it's a library bug**: both queries are pure salsa queries (inputs +
+tracked functions with `cycle_fn`/`cycle_initial`, no untracked reads), so per
+the assertion's own message this is salsa's responsibility. In release builds
+the same condition only logs a warning and `revisions.changed_at` silently
+moves backwards (`src/function/backdate.rs:54-58`), which can make downstream
+memos treat a changed dependency as unchanged — i.e. potential stale results,
+not just a noisy assert.
+
+**Code path**: `IngredientImpl::backdate_if_appropriate` ->
+`MemoHeader::backdate` -> `report_backdate_violation`
+(`src/function/backdate.rs:76`, panic under `cfg(debug_assertions)`).
+Mechanism: `MemoHeader::can_backdate` (`src/function/backdate.rs:33-46`)
+refuses to backdate memos whose new revisions carry cycle heads — the "bandaid"
+described in the TODO comment there. In R3, the cycle head's (`infer_use`)
+final memo is backdated to R2 (equal value), but the cycle participant's
+(`infer_definition`) memo is *not* backdated (its revisions still carry the
+cycle head) and keeps `changed_at = R3`. When the cycle is broken in R4,
+`infer_definition` re-executes, its dependencies now say "last changed at R2"
+(base edge R2, backdated `infer_use` R2), and backdating the equal value trips
+`old_changed_at (R3) > new_changed_at (R2)`. This is exactly the scenario the
+`can_backdate` TODO comment asks for a test to demonstrate.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None — no property was asserted and then found to be over-claiming a contract
+salsa never made. Related decisions worth flagging:
+
+- The dataflow machine's `no_cycle_broken` restriction is a *known-bug
+  exclusion* (referencing the canonical failing test), not a soundness fix; it
+  should be removed once the backdate bug is fixed.
+- I considered asserting `Durability::NEVER_CHANGE` for `synthetic_write` and
+  for re-setting frozen fields; both panic *by documented contract*, so the
+  generators avoid them (freezing itself is still exercised in the crown
+  machine).
+- Fixture-protecting bounds (u32 sums in `durability.rs`, accumulator counts in
+  `accumulate.rs`, dataflow increments) are commented in the tests as protecting
+  the test fixture/resources, not salsa's contract.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill and the Rust reference worked well — everything compiled
+almost first-try and the workflow (explore -> evidence -> properties -> run and
+reflect) led straight to a real bug. Specific notes:
+
+- **Accurate**: the MSRV gotcha (`cargo add` fails against salsa's
+  `rust-version = 1.85`; `--ignore-rust-version` per the reference worked), the
+  package-vs-crate naming (`hegeltest` / `hegel`), `sampled_from` over owned
+  enums (`Option<Durability>`), state-machine syntax, `tc.assume` for
+  inapplicable rules, and the "no global case-count override" note (I had to
+  sed `test_cases` for the 10x pass exactly as predicted).
+- **Gap — invariant vs. check rule**: SKILL.md's model-test pseudocode puts the
+  subject-vs-model comparison in an `invariant`, which runs after *every* rule.
+  For incremental-computation subjects this is actively harmful: querying
+  everything after every step means no revision is ever skipped, masking the
+  lazy-validation code paths where salsa bugs live. I used a `#[rule]
+  check_against_fresh_db` instead so hegel generates runs where several
+  mutations happen between checks. A sentence in the stateful-testing section
+  ("if checking itself perturbs the subject — caches, lazy validation — make
+  the check a rule, not an invariant") would have saved reasoning time.
+- **Minor cosmetic**: a machine with no `#[invariant]`s still prints "Initial
+  invariant check." in the failure trace, which briefly confused the step-trace
+  reading.
+- **Nice**: the shrunk failure trace (`Step N: <rule>` plus named draws) made
+  deriving the hand-written 3-revision reproducer from the 6-step shrunk
+  machine run straightforward. Shrinking of the first dataflow failure took
+  ~8s, well within the reference's expectations.
+- **One reference nit**: for interned/input struct accessors the reference has
+  no note that salsa's `#[returns(ref)]`-style accessors return `&String`, so
+  the obvious `assert_eq!(x.data(&db), data)` needs `&data`. Trivial compile
+  fix, but a "salsa-like accessor return modes" example under the equality
+  gotchas would fit.
